  "narrative": "The enrichment results, pathway memberships, and KEGG reaction-graph paths provide sufficient evidence. I will now write the narrative.\n\n---\n\nThe eight differentially-abundant metabolites map overwhelmingly to **Arachidonic Acid Metabolism** (KEGG map00590; SMP00075). This pathway anchors every other top hit — the five NSAID/pharmacology \"action pathway\" entries (Acetylsalicylic Acid, Rofecoxib, Salicylate-sodium, Acetaminophen, Antipyrine) are all drug-interaction overlays on the same arachidonic acid scaffold. Across those six enriched SMPDB entries the matched compounds are identical: C00219, C04805, C14717, C14768, C04742, C00909 — a 95 % overlap with the input list.\n\n**Key drivers.** Arachidonic acid (C00219) is the primary upstream substrate. KEGG reaction-graph analysis confirms it is reachable in two enzymatic steps to Leukotriene A4 (C00909) and to 5-HETE (C04805), confirming it as a common precursor for the leukotriene and hydroxy-eicosatetraenoic acid (HETE) branches. 15-Deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂ (C14717) and 5,6-epoxy-8,11,14-eicosatrienoic acid (C14768) occupy the cyclopentenone-prostaglandin and epoxy-eicosatrienoic acid branches, respectively, consistent with prostaglandin-endoperoxide synthase (COX) and lipoxygenase (LOX) metabolism of the same pool of free arachidonate. 3,4-Dihydroxybenzeneacetic acid (C01161) and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid (C00156) likely reflect downstream phenolic antioxidants or phase-II conjugates of downstream aromatic products.\n\n**Biological significance.** Coordinated elevation of six arachidonic acid–derived lipid mediators suggests the treatment perturbs phospholipase-mediated release of free arachidonate and the balance between COX/LOX pathways. Persistent elevation of leukotrienes (via LTA₄) would promote neutrophilic inflammation, while surplus 15-HETE and 5-HETE are associated with prostanoid amplification and oxidative-stress signaling. The cyclopentenone prostaglandin 15d-PGJ₂, though often anti-inflammatory via Nrf₂ activation, can be pro-apoptotic at high local concentrations. The hydroxybenzoic acid species hint at compensatory antioxidant responses. Together, the data point to a broad dysregulation of the arachidonic acid cascade with downstream consequences for inflammation resolution, cell survival, and redox homeostasis.",
